<Page
  route="approach"
  title="Our approach: AI for autonomy"
  heading="Our approach"
  description="A voice assistant, non-invasive EEG and EMG signal decoding, then rehabilitation hardware: the three steps of Neura Mobilis."
  lead="We start with software, the simplest and most accessible, before moving to hardware. Three steps, each built on the previous one."
>
  <h2>1. Mobilis, the voice assistant</h2>
  <p>
    Understanding a request in natural language and acting on the phone. This is what we do today, in
    testing. <a href={routes.mobilis.en}>Learn more about Mobilis</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Signal decoding</h2>
  <p>
    When a person intends to move, their body emits signals, even when the movement does not happen. We
    want to capture them with non-invasive sensors, without surgery: electroencephalography (EEG) on the
    scalp and electromyography (EMG) on the muscles. An AI interprets them and triggers a precise action:
    controlling a device, or amplifying the signal to assist the movement.
  </p>
  <p>
    The challenge is recognising an intention in a noisy signal that differs from one person to the next.
    This step is at the research stage: we are starting with open datasets.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Rehabilitation hardware</h2>
  <p>
    In time, this AI could drive rehabilitation devices, such as a walking exoskeleton or stimulators
    placed on the muscles. The hardware would be provided by a medical centre, which monitors and adjusts
    its use.
  </p>

  <h2>What we hold ourselves to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Health data is sensitive.</strong> We only collect what we are allowed and need to collect, and
      we anonymise or pseudonymise it as soon as possible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A medical device must be certified.</strong> A product that assists or rehabilitates movement
      falls under the EU Medical Device Regulation. That is why we start with Mobilis, an accessibility
      tool with no medical purpose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>AI stays under human control.</strong> Our systems always remain under the control of the person
      and their caregiver, as the EU AI Act requires for high-risk uses.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Page>
